Figure: 7_2

The previous interaction plan had issues.
Plan-level fixes requested:
  • Revise the plan so the source-faithful invariant diagram is the primary default view, with the code-tracing workbench offered only as an optional interactive extension.

Generation-level issues observed:
  • Make the default state match the source: use the abstract 16-cell array with p, i, j, r at the same boundaries and no numeric cell contents except the pivot x.
  • Remove or hide trace-specific highlights, captions, frame counters, and code-editor chrome from the default visual so it can serve as a static figure replacement.
  • Place a large italic x inside the final pivot cell instead of showing a numeric value with 'x=4' below the array.
  • Enlarge the main schematic and adjust brace/label placement so the ≤x, >x, and unrestricted labels match the prominence and spacing of the source.

Specific scores:
  • Overall: 3.6/5
  • Concept accuracy: 4/5
  • Scale-Wrong
  • Hallucination

Revise the interaction plan to address these issues.
Output ONLY valid JSON (no markdown, no explanation).

Previous plan (for reference):
{
  "concept": "During PARTITION, the indices i and j maintain the invariant that A[p..i] contains values ≤ x, A[i+1..j-1] contains values > x, A[j..r-1] is not yet examined, and A[r] stores the pivot x.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "single horizontal array strip divided into equal rectangular cells from index p through index r",
    "left index label p above the first cell",
    "index label i above the boundary after the ≤ x region",
    "index label j above the boundary before the unrestricted region",
    "index label r above the pivot position at the right end",
    "heavy black vertical boundary marker after the A[p..i] region",
    "heavy black vertical boundary marker before index j",
    "heavy black vertical boundary marker before the pivot cell A[r]",
    "pale gray shaded region A[p..i] labeled ≤ x",
    "darker gray shaded region A[i+1..j-1] labeled > x",
    "white unshaded region A[j..r-1] labeled unrestricted",
    "rightmost pivot cell A[r] containing italic x",
    "curly brace under the pale gray region with label ≤ x",
    "curly brace under the darker gray region with label > x",
    "curly brace under the unrestricted white region with label unrestricted",
    "current comparison highlight on A[j] during replay",
    "swap highlight connecting A[i] and A[j] when the ≤ x branch is taken",
    "final pivot-swap highlight between A[i+1] and A[r]",
    "inline invariant annotation displayed above or below the strip during execution"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "partitionCode",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "label": "Implement PARTITION(A, p, r)",
      "entry_point": "PARTITION(A, p, r)",
      "api": [
        {
          "op": "pivot(r)",
          "does": "returns A[r] and records it visually as the pivot value x"
        },
        {
          "op": "compare(j, x)",
          "does": "true when A[j] <= x; highlights A[j] and the pivot value being compared"
        },
        {
          "op": "swap(i, j)",
          "does": "exchange A[i] and A[j] — the ONLY way to mutate the array"
        },
        {
          "op": "setI(i)",
          "does": "moves the i boundary after index i; use p - 1 when the ≤ x region is empty"
        },
        {
          "op": "setJ(j)",
          "does": "moves the j boundary to the first unrestricted index"
        },
        {
          "op": "note(text)",
          "does": "adds a short annotation to the current replay frame"
        }
      ],
      "frame": "the whole subarray A[p..r] as a horizontal strip, with p, i, j, r labels, shaded invariant regions, the current compared cell, and any swap or note produced by the last operation",
      "input": {
        "label": "Initial array A[p..r]",
        "default": "2, 8, 7, 1, 3, 5, 6, 4"
      },
      "sample_code": "function PARTITION(A, p, r) {\n  const x = pivot(r);\n  let i = p - 1;\n  setI(i);\n\n  for (let j = p; j <= r - 1; j++) {\n    setJ(j);\n    note('Top of loop: left is <= x, middle is > x, right is unrestricted.');\n\n    if (compare(j, x)) {\n      i = i + 1;\n      swap(i, j);\n      setI(i);\n      note('A[j] <= x, so it is swapped into the <= x region.');\n    } else {\n      note('A[j] > x, so it stays in the > x region.');\n    }\n\n    setJ(j + 1);\n    note('After the iteration, j advances and the invariant is restored.');\n  }\n\n  swap(i + 1, r);\n  setI(i + 1);\n  note('Place the pivot between the <= x and > x regions.');\n  return i + 1;\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function PARTITION(A, p, r) {\n  const x = pivot(r);\n  let i = p - 1;\n  setI(i);\n\n  for (let j = p; j <= r - 1; j++) {\n    setJ(j);\n    if (compare(j, x)) {\n      swap(i, j);\n      setI(i);\n      i = i + 1;\n      note('Bug: swaps before advancing i, so the new <= x item goes to the wrong place.');\n    }\n    setJ(j + 1);\n  }\n\n  swap(i + 1, r);\n  setI(i + 1);\n  return i + 1;\n}",
      "success_check": "the returned pivot index q satisfies A[q] = x, every element in A[p..q-1] <= x, and every element in A[q+1..r] > x",
      "teaches": "The learner understands that PARTITION is not sorting the subarray; it is preserving three moving regions and using swaps only when the current item belongs in the ≤ x region"
    },
    {
      "id": "invariantCheckpoint",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Invariant checkpoint",
      "states": [
        "top of iteration",
        "after <= x branch",
        "after > x branch",
        "after final pivot swap"
      ],
      "default": "top of iteration",
      "teaches": "The learner understands what must be true before and after each loop iteration for the partition invariant to remain valid"
    },
    {
      "id": "currentCase",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Current A[j] case",
      "options": [
        "A[j] <= x",
        "A[j] > x"
      ],
      "default": "A[j] <= x",
      "teaches": "The learner understands why the ≤ x case expands the left region by swapping and advancing i, while the > x case only lets j move past the current element"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Read the invariant",
      "narration": "At the top of the loop, everything up to i is already known to be ≤ x, everything from i+1 through j-1 is known to be > x, and everything from j through r-1 has not yet been examined.",
      "state": {
        "invariantCheckpoint": "top of iteration",
        "currentCase": "A[j] <= x"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "When A[j] is small enough",
      "narration": "If the current element is ≤ x, it belongs in the left region. PARTITION first expands that region by one position, then swaps A[j] into it.",
      "state": {
        "invariantCheckpoint": "after <= x branch",
        "currentCase": "A[j] <= x"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "When A[j] is too large",
      "narration": "If the current element is > x, it already belongs in the middle region. No swap is needed; advancing j simply grows the > x region by one cell.",
      "state": {
        "invariantCheckpoint": "after > x branch",
        "currentCase": "A[j] > x"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Place the pivot",
      "narration": "After every non-pivot element has been classified, the pivot swaps into position i+1. That position separates the final ≤ x region from the final > x region.",
      "state": {
        "invariantCheckpoint": "after final pivot swap",
        "currentCase": "A[j] <= x"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The moving boundary j discovers elements, while i marks the end of the ≤ x region; the invariant is restored after every iteration by either swapping a small element left or simply advancing past a large one.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the figure as a long, flat, textbook-style array strip with black outlines and equal cells. Use pale gray for A[p..i], darker gray for A[i+1..j-1], white for A[j..r-1], and a white pivot cell at the far right containing italic x. Labels p, i, j, and r should appear in large italic serif math style above the strip, with thick black vertical markers at the region boundaries. Curly braces under the first three regions should update as i and j move and retain the labels ≤ x, > x, and unrestricted. During code replay, show numeric array values inside the cells while keeping the same region shading so learners can connect the abstract invariant to the concrete swaps."
}

